          <a href="#universal" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Universal and Third-Party Bags</a>
          <a href="#wet" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Wet Pickup Rules</a>
          <a href="#fine-dust" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Drywall, Concrete and Ash</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cost Per Job</a>
          <a href="#small-vacs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Small and Cordless Vacs</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most shop vac bags fail for one boring reason: they were bought for the wrong drum. A bag that does not seal around the inlet lets dust straight past it into the cartridge, and you pay for a consumable that does nothing. So this guide starts with fitment, by brand and tank size, and only then gets to filtration grades. Get the part number right and a $10 bag will do more for suction than a $40 filter.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="bag-or-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. When a Bag Earns Its Cost (and When It Doesn't)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A collection bag is a second filter that sits in front of your cartridge and takes the debris before the pleats ever see it. Our <a href="/shop-vac-filters-explained/">shop vac filter guide</a> covers how cartridges blind and why; this page assumes you already know you want a bag and need to choose one. The short version of the decision is that a bag pays for itself on any job where the dust is finer than flour and would otherwise coat the pleats in minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those jobs are predictable. Sanding joint compound, cutting cement board, cleaning up after a tile saw, emptying a cold wood stove, sweeping up the fine grey layer after a concrete grinder, vacuuming a furnace room or the dust shelf above a drop ceiling. In each case the material is fine enough to load a cartridge quickly and fine enough that dumping an open drum outside puts a fair amount of it straight back into the air you are breathing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The jobs where a bag wastes money are just as predictable. Leaves, mulch, wood chips from a planer, gravel, packing peanuts and the general detritus of a garage clean-out are coarse. They fall to the bottom of the drum and never trouble the filter, and they fill a bag to its limit in a fraction of the time, so you end up throwing away a $10 bag that is full of material a bare drum would have handled for free. For that kind of work, run the cartridge alone and empty the drum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is water. Every bag on this list is labelled for dry pickup only. Paper and synthetic bags are not built to hold liquid, and running one in a wet job ruins it and can choke airflow. If your week alternates between a flooded basement and drywall repair, the bag goes in and out with the job, and we cover the changeover routine further down.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bag the fine stuff, skip it for the coarse stuff, never bag water.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the debris would cloud the air when you tip the drum, use a bag. If it would just clatter into the bin, don't. If it is wet, take the bag out and fit the foam sleeve.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Chose These Six
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the five drum brands most American owners actually have in the garage: RIDGID from Home Depot, Shop-Vac, Craftsman, DEWALT and Vacmaster. Each brand uses its own inlet collar and its own sizing, so a bag roundup that lists one "best" bag for everyone is not useful. Instead we picked the bag that matches each brand's most common drum sizes, plus a HEPA-rated option for owners who are cleaning silica dust or allergens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only manufacturer parts made the list. There are dozens of aftermarket packs on the market that claim to replace a RIDGID or Shop-Vac part number, and some are perfectly decent. But their collar dimensions, materials and seams vary from seller to seller, and none of them publish filtration data either. Where we recommend a genuine part, the maker has a published compatibility list we can point you to, which matters more than anything else when the job is getting a bag to seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things. First, fitment: which tank sizes and model numbers the maker lists. Second, filtration grade: whether the bag is sold as a standard dust bag, a high-efficiency bag or a HEPA-rated bag, and what, if anything, the maker says about fine debris like drywall dust or cold ash. Third, pack size and the rough per-bag cost at typical retail. Fourth, what the label says about wet use and hazardous material, because every one of these bags carries restrictions and you should know them before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out products that appear to be discontinued or that we could not find sold under their current part number. Craftsman's fine-dust bag for 16 and 20 gallon drums, CMXZVBE38750, is a good product on paper, but we could not find the genuine part listed reliably, so the general-purpose CMXZVBE38749 takes the Craftsman slot. RIDGID's smaller VF3501 bag for 3 to 4.5 gallon drums is similar: genuine stock was hard to find, while third-party copies were everywhere.
          </p>
        </div>

