        <div id="fitment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Bag to Your Drum Size and Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vac bags are not universal, and the size printed on the box is only half the information you need. Each brand designs the bag's cardboard or plastic collar around its own inlet, which is a tube that pokes into the drum from the side on most machines. The collar slides over or onto that tube and makes the seal. A collar that is a few millimetres loose leaks; one that is too tight tears when you fit it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the label on your vac, not the box it came in. Most drums carry a sticker with the model number on the motor head or the back of the tank. Search that number against the bag maker's compatibility list. RIDGID's VF3502 page, for example, says the bag fits most RIDGID 12 to 16 gallon vacs but names WD1280, WD1636 and WD1637 as exceptions. That kind of exclusion list is exactly what a generic "fits 12 to 16 gallon" description leaves out.
          </p>

