          <p>
            Shop-Vac is the most confusing brand because it uses letter types and several item numbers for the same bag. The high-efficiency series runs Type H for 5 to 8 gallon drums, Type I for 10 to 14 gallon and Type J for 15 to 22 gallon, and each is sold under more than one item number depending on pack size and retailer. Shop-Vac's own page for the Type J bag lists 9067300 and 9067333 among the numbers it replaces. Shop-Vac also notes that these bags fit vacs with the inlet on the tank, not models with the inlet in the lid, and that the 5 to 8 gallon Type H does not fit its wall-mount or HangUp vacs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Craftsman drums sold since the brand moved to Stanley Black and Decker carry CMXEVBE model numbers and take CMXZVBE bags. An older Sears-era Craftsman vac is a different machine with a different inlet and needs a different part, so check the model number before you order. DEWALT's DXV-series drums are similar: the DXVA19-4102 is listed for the DXV12P, DXV14P, DXV16P, DXV16S and DXV16PA, and the 6 to 10 gallon DXVA19-4101 for the smaller drums in that line.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own a brand we have not covered, or a vac from a big-box house brand, the same method applies. Find the model number, find the maker's own bag, and only then look at whether a cheaper copy is worth the risk. If your vac never shipped with a bag option at all, which is true of some compact and wall-mounted machines, a bag is not the answer; a fine-dust or HEPA cartridge is.
          </p>
