        <div id="grades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Standard vs High Efficiency vs HEPA Bags
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bag makers sell three grades, and the labels are less precise than they look. A standard bag, like Vacmaster's VDBL or DEWALT's DXVA19-4102, is a paper or poly bag designed to catch general dry debris and the coarser fraction of dust. A high-efficiency bag, like RIDGID's VF3502 or Shop-Vac's Type J, is sold specifically for fine dust and is usually a multi-layer synthetic or treated media. A HEPA bag, like Shop-Vac's 9021833, is sold as stopping ultra-fine dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            What none of these makers publish, on the pages we checked, is a micron rating or a capture percentage for the bag. Shop-Vac's HEPA bag page says "HEPA filtration stops ultra fine dust" and gives no number. RIDGID calls its bag high efficiency and leaves it there. That is not a reason to distrust the products, but it does mean you cannot compare grades across brands on paper. A high-efficiency bag from one maker is not certified to be equal to another's.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also means a HEPA bag does not make your vac a HEPA vac. A shop vac's sealing at the lid, the motor head and the hose cuff was not designed as a sealed HEPA system, and fine dust that slips past a gasket bypasses the bag entirely. The practical value of a HEPA bag is that it stops much more of the fine fraction before it reaches the cartridge, which keeps your cartridge and your exhaust cleaner. If you are doing regulated silica work, such as cutting or grinding concrete for a living, you need a dust extractor sold to meet that standard; our <a href="/shop-vac-vs-dust-extractor/">shop vac vs dust extractor guide</a> explains the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner, our advice is simple. Use a high-efficiency bag for drywall, sanding and general renovation dust, because that is what those bags are sold for. Step up to a HEPA bag, paired with a HEPA cartridge, when the dust is mineral, such as concrete, tile, grout or mortar, or when someone in the house has allergies or asthma. Use a standard bag for ordinary garage cleaning where the goal is mostly easier, cleaner emptying.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
