        <div id="universal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Universal and Third-Party Bags: When They're Fine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for any of the part numbers on this page and you will find aftermarket packs selling for less per bag than the genuine article, often in packs of six to twenty. Some of these are made well. The trouble is that you cannot tell which ones from a listing, because the collar dimensions and the media are not standardised and the filtration claims are not independently rated.
          </p>

          <p>
            The thing to look for is the collar. A third-party bag with a rigid collar sized to the same inlet as the genuine part will usually seal. One with a soft rubber or elastic "universal" collar designed to stretch over several inlet sizes is the one most likely to leak, pop off mid-job, or tear at the seam where the collar meets the bag. Owners commonly report that the cheapest universal bags slip off the inlet once they get heavy, which is the worst failure, because the bag then sits in the drum while dust goes straight to the cartridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule is to buy the genuine part for fine-dust jobs and treat aftermarket bags as fine for coarse, low-stakes work. If the bag's job is to keep drywall dust off your cartridge and out of your lungs, the few extra dollars buy a known fit. If the bag's job is to make emptying sawdust from a miter saw station a bit tidier, a good third-party pack is a reasonable saving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, check the fit the first time. Fit the bag, run the vac for ten seconds with the lid on, then switch it off and look: the bag should have inflated evenly and the collar should still be fully seated on the inlet. After the first full job, take the cartridge out and look at it. If it is visibly dusty on the outside when the bag was supposed to be taking the load, the bag is leaking at the collar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wet Pickup Rules: Bags Out, Sleeve On
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every bag on this list is either labelled for dry pickup only or, in the case of the Shop-Vac HEPA bag, does not claim any wet use. Treat them all as dry-only. A paper bag soaks through and splits; a synthetic bag holds water against the media, gets heavy, and can pull off the inlet. Either way, you end up with a soggy mess in the drum and possibly a choked cartridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            The changeover from dry to wet takes about a minute. Switch off and unplug the vac. Lift off the lid, remove the bag carefully by sliding the collar off the inlet, and close the collar flap if it has one so the bag does not spill while you carry it out. Remove the pleated cartridge too, because most shop vac cartridges are also dry-only. Fit the foam sleeve if your model uses one, then do the wet job. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/">vacuuming water with a shop vac</a> covers the rest of that routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going back to dry work is where people damage things. After a wet job, the drum and the inlet tube are still damp, and a dry bag fitted straight away wicks moisture into its base and collar. Dry the drum with a rag, leave the lid off for an hour if you can, and fit the bag only when the inside of the tank is dry to the touch. The same is true of the cartridge, which should never go back in while the drum is wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            A related mistake is using the bag to pick up damp material, such as wet sawdust from a rainy deck job or mud tracked into a garage. Damp debris is still wet as far as a paper bag is concerned. It clumps, holds moisture against the media and can rot the bag in storage. Let it dry first, or pick it up with the bag out and accept a messier empty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fine-dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Drywall, Concrete and Ash: How to Use a Bag on the Hard Jobs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drywall dust is the job bags were invented for. Joint compound sands into a powder so fine that it passes through ordinary paper and loads a standard cartridge in a few minutes of sanding. With a high-efficiency bag fitted, the bag takes the bulk of it and the cartridge stays usable. Expect a bag to fill faster than you think, though, because fine dust packs densely: on a big sanding job you may fill a bag well before the drum looks full.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Change the bag on suction, not on volume</h3>

          <p>
            The tell that a bag is full is not how heavy it feels but how much suction you have at the nozzle. As the bag's media loads with fine dust, airflow drops just as it would with a blinded cartridge. If suction falls off noticeably during a job, switch off and change the bag rather than pushing on. A bag that is fully loaded can also start passing dust or split at the seam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete, tile, grout and mortar dust contain crystalline silica, which is a respiratory hazard rather than a nuisance. For this work, pair a HEPA bag with a HEPA cartridge, wear a properly fitted respirator, and do not dump the drum indoors. The bag is what makes this tolerable, because you lift out a closed bag instead of tipping a cloud of silica into a garbage can. If you do this kind of cutting often, look at a proper dust extractor rather than relying on a shop vac.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ash is the one place where a bag can be actively dangerous. Shop-Vac lists cold ash as an intended use for its Type J bag, and the word cold matters. Embers can survive in a bed of ash for days, and a hidden ember in a paper or synthetic bag inside a plastic drum is a fire. Only vacuum ash that has been out for several days and is cold to the touch all the way through, and if you clean a wood stove or fireplace every week, buy a metal ash vacuum built for the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            For workshop sawdust, a bag is optional. Fine sanding dust from a random orbital sander justifies one; chips and shavings from a table saw or planer do not. If your vac doubles as dust collection for a tool, check whether the tool's port sends fine dust or coarse chips before you commit to buying bags by the dozen. Our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac buyer's guide</a> covers which machines handle tool-triggered dust collection well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Bags Cost Per Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vac bags are not cheap per unit. At typical retail, a genuine high-efficiency or HEPA bag for a 12 to 20 gallon drum sits somewhere around $12 to $18 each, and a standard bag around $5 to $8. Shop-Vac lists its Type J two-pack at $30.99 and its HEPA two-pack at $36.33 on its own site, and the RIDGID VF3502 two-pack is commonly listed in the high twenties. Prices move, so treat these as a guide to the tier rather than a quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful comparison is not the bag's price but what it protects. A fine-dust or HEPA cartridge for a mid-size drum often costs $25 to $70. On a drywall job without a bag, that cartridge can lose most of its airflow in a single session and may need to be cleaned several times or replaced. With a bag taking the load, the same cartridge can last many months. One bag per serious fine-dust job is usually a good trade.
          </p>

