                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leaves, gravel, debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wastes the bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water or wet debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bags are dry-only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The way to keep cost down is to match the bag to the job and not to use one by habit. A vac that lives in a garage and picks up leaves, sawdust and spilled potting soil can go months without a bag, with the cartridge tapped out now and then. The same vac brought indoors for a bathroom renovation should have a high-efficiency bag in it for the whole project. Buying bags in larger packs brings the per-bag price down, which is worth it only if you do this kind of work regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not try to save money by emptying and reusing a disposable bag. The collar flap and seams are not designed for it, the media is already loaded, and tipping a full bag of drywall dust into a bin to reuse it undoes the main benefit, which is never having to see that dust again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-vacs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Bags for Small, Cordless and Older Vacs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compact vacs are where bag fitment gets tricky. Many 2 to 6 gallon machines use their own small bag type, some cordless vacs offer no bag at all, and some wall-mounted units only take a cartridge. If you are shopping for a <a href="/best-small-shop-vac/">small shop vac</a> and expect to use it on fine dust, check that the maker sells a bag for that exact model before you buy the vac, because it is a far bigger factor in day-to-day usefulness than an extra half horsepower.
          </p>

          <p>
            On small drums the capacity problem is worse. A 4 gallon bag fills very quickly with drywall dust, and a small machine's lower airflow loses suction sooner as the bag media loads. For a one-room job that is fine. For a whole-house sanding project, a 12 to 16 gallon drum with a high-efficiency bag is a much less frustrating tool, because you change bags a fraction as often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older machines are a different problem: the bag the vac shipped with may no longer be made. Shop-Vac has kept its letter types consistent for many years, so an older Shop-Vac with the inlet on the tank will often take a current bag of the right type. For older RIDGID, Craftsman or house-brand machines, look up the original part number and search for it rather than trusting a size range, and be prepared to fall back to a fine-dust cartridge if no bag is still sold for that inlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever drum you have, the attachments matter as much as the bag on fine-dust work. A wide floor tool and a brush nozzle cover more surface per pass and keep the hose from dragging through dust you have not picked up yet. Our guide to the <a href="/best-shop-vac-attachments/">best shop vac attachments</a> covers which ones are worth adding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Gallon Size Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two bags both labelled 12 to 16 gallon can have different collars, and RIDGID lists specific 12 to 16 gallon models its own bag does not fit. Look up your vac's model number against the bag maker's compatibility list before ordering, especially if you are buying a large pack you cannot return once opened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Bag In for Wet Pickup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every bag here is dry-only or not rated for wet use. Water soaks a paper bag through and loads a synthetic one until it pulls off the inlet. Remove the bag and the pleated cartridge, fit the foam sleeve, and dry the drum completely before the bag goes back in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Bag Until the Drum Looks Full</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine dust packs densely and loads the bag's media long before the bag fills the drum. Change the bag when suction at the nozzle drops noticeably, not when it looks full. A fully loaded bag starts choking airflow, and an overfilled one can split at the seam.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Ash That Is Only Warm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Embers can survive inside a bed of ash for days, and a paper or synthetic bag in a plastic drum is fuel. Only vacuum ash that has been out for several days and is cold all the way through, and use a metal ash vacuum if you clean a stove or fireplace every week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Bag for Coarse Debris</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaves, chips, gravel and garage clean-out debris never trouble a cartridge but fill a bag in minutes, so you throw away a $10 to $18 bag full of material a bare drum handles for free. Save bags for fine dust and empty the drum for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are shop vac bags universal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Each brand designs its bag collar around its own inlet, and sizes overlap without matching. A RIDGID Size A bag, a Shop-Vac Type J and a DEWALT DXVA19-4102 are not interchangeable even where the gallon ranges overlap. Some aftermarket bags claim universal fit with stretchy collars, but those are the ones most likely to leak or slip off the inlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a filter if I use a bag?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The bag catches debris before the cartridge, but the cartridge still filters the air before it reaches the motor. Running a bag without a cartridge sends anything that passes the bag into the motor and out the exhaust. Keep the cartridge in for all dry pickup, and remove both for wet pickup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use shop vac bags for water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every bag on this list is labelled for dry pickup only or carries no wet rating. Paper bags soak through and split, and synthetic bags hold water until they pull off the inlet. Remove the bag and the pleated cartridge before wet pickup, fit the foam sleeve if your model uses one, and dry the drum before refitting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between high efficiency and HEPA shop vac bags?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-efficiency bags are sold for fine dust like drywall and sanding dust. HEPA bags are sold as capturing ultra-fine dust and are the step up for silica or allergens. Neither RIDGID nor Shop-Vac publishes a micron rating for these bags, so treat HEPA as the higher grade rather than a certified figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you change a shop vac bag?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Change it when suction drops noticeably, not when it looks full. On drywall sanding that can be a single large room, because fine dust loads the media quickly. On sawdust, a bag may last several sessions. Never empty and reuse a disposable bag; the collar flap and seams are not built for it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do shop vac bags reduce suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slightly, compared with a clean cartridge alone, because air passes through an extra layer. In practice they usually improve suction across a job, because they keep the cartridge from blinding. On fine dust, a bagged vac holds its suction far longer than a bagless one, which loses airflow quickly as the pleats load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap third-party shop vac bags any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are fine for coarse, low-stakes work like sawdust. The risk is the collar: universal or loose collars leak or slip off when the bag gets heavy, which sends dust straight to the cartridge. For drywall, concrete or ash, buy the genuine part for your model so the fit is known.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

